{"id":"h184","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A particle executing simple harmonic motion given by y = 4 sin {(2\\pit / 6) + \\alpha} is displaced +1 unit when t = 0. Find: (a) the phase angle when t = 0; (b) the difference in phase between any two positions of the particle 2 seconds apart? (c) the phase angle corresponding to a displacement of +2; (d) the time necessary to reach a displacement of +3 from the initial position.\nIs the correct answer \"Phase angle at t = 0: π/8 radians, Phase difference between two positions 2 seconds apart: 45 degrees, Phase angle corresponding to a displacement of +2: 11.25 degrees, Time to reach a displacement of +3: 1.52 seconds\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The motion is y = 4 sin(πt/3 + α) with ω = π/3 rad/s and period 6 s.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The initial phase is α = arcsin(1/4) ≈ 14.5°, not π/8.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"π/8 rad would give sin ≈ 0.383, inconsistent with the initial displacement of 1 unit.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The phase difference over 2 seconds is 2π/3 rad = 120°, not 45°.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A displacement of +2 corresponds to a phase of 30° (or 150°), not 11.25°.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Going from displacement +1 to +3 changes the phase by about 34.1°, taking about 0.57 s, not 1.52 s.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The proposed answer might correspond to a different convention or reading of phase, but this is dismissed.","confidence":0.05,"lean":1,"theta":0.0785,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Each of parts (a) through (d) of the proposed answer fails a direct check against the equation, so the stated answer is incorrect.","confidence":0